From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:46:58 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: dma: enable descriptor fetch-on-demand last, not first

dma_ctrl_cfg() turned descriptor fetch-on-demand on partway through the
controller's global configuration, and dma_ctrl_init() calls dma_ctrl_cfg()
as its second step -- right after dma_ctrl_reset(), and well before the
per-port and per-channel loops that program dma_port_cfg() and
dma_chan_cfg(). So between DMA_CTRL_DS_FOD going up and the last channel
being configured, the controller is entitled to fetch descriptors from
channels whose descriptor base, length and control words are still whatever
the reset left behind.

AVM's 08.25 GPL release closes that window by moving the block: it is
deleted from dma_ctrl_cfg() and re-added verbatim as the final statement
of dma_ctrl_init(), after the loops (hdma.c:3026-3030 in Linux 4.9.337,
against :587-591 inside dma_ctrl_cfg in Linux 4.9.198). Do the same
here. The block moves with its "int enable" declaration, which
dma_ctrl_init() now needs and dma_ctrl_cfg() still has for its other
five flag tests.

"Final statement of dma_ctrl_init()" does not land in the same place for
us as for AVM, because our dma_ctrl_init() hosts two calls AVM keeps in
the probe -- dma_irq_chip_init() and tasklet_init(). AVM's probe runs
dma_irq_chip_init (hdma.c:4392), then dma_ctrl_init (:4398) with the FOD
block at its tail (:3030), then tasklet_init (:4400). So:

  - Against dma_irq_chip_init we match AVM. FOD still comes after it, and
    there is no reason to let it precede an IRQ chip that is about to start
    delivering channel interrupts.

  - Against tasklet_init we invert AVM, deliberately. AVM initialises
    the tasklet only after dma_ctrl_init has already run
    devm_request_irq for every channel, so a channel interrupt arriving
    in that window would find an uninitialised tasklet to schedule. This
    port moves tasklet_init ahead of the request_irq loop for exactly
    that reason, which necessarily puts it ahead of the FOD block too.

Moving FOD to the tail also puts it behind two probe-failure early
returns it used to sit ahead of: hdma.c:1276 when dma_irq_chip_init()
fails, and :1308 when devm_request_irq() fails. Both abort the probe, so
the controller is torn down with FOD left at its post-reset default and
no descriptor fetching is armed against half-configured channels -- the
failure path ends up in a safer state than before, not a worse one.

The flag is live on both boards, so this is a real window rather than a
theoretical one. DMA_FTOD comes from the "lantiq,dma-desc-fod" DT property
(hdma.c:1112-1117) and the controller nodes carry it in both releases.

dma_ctrl_cfg() has a second caller: hdma_ig192_toe_dma3_reset(), the TOE
reset-parity path, which drives a hand-built cid=DMA3 controller through
the same primitives without going near dma_ctrl_init(). Losing the FOD call
there changes nothing, and provably so rather than by argument:
dma_ctrl_desc_fetch_on_demand_cfg() returns immediately for DMA0, DMA3 and
DMA4 (hdma.c:401-403, an early return restored because the vendor skips the
write for those cids). DMA3 was never getting a write from that call. Its
comment is updated to say that the path no longer reaches the function at
all, so the reasoning survives the next reader.

That early return itself is untouched -- this commit only moves a call
site.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/dma/hdma.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/dma/hdma.c
index b57d7372275d..a0c652352765 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/dma/hdma.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/dma/hdma.c
@@ -284,8 +284,15 @@ static void dma_ctrl_global_polling_enable(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 }
 
 /*
- * dma_ctrl_desc_fetch_on_demand_cfg — AVM hdma.c:494-509. DMA1TX / DMA1RX /
- * DMA2TX fall through as before.
+ * dma_ctrl_desc_fetch_on_demand_cfg - AVM hdma.c:494-509.
+ *
+ * Since AVM 08.25 moved the call out of dma_ctrl_cfg and onto the tail of
+ * dma_ctrl_init, the TOE reset-parity path (hdma_ig192_toe_dma3_reset,
+ * cid=DMA3) no longer reaches this function at all — it calls dma_ctrl_cfg
+ * directly, not dma_ctrl_init. That path's hardware effect is unchanged
+ * either way, precisely because this early return meant DMA3 never got a
+ * write. Kept because AVM has it. DMA1TX / DMA1RX / DMA2TX fall through as
+ * before.
  */
 static void dma_ctrl_desc_fetch_on_demand_cfg(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl,
 					      int enable)
@@ -375,7 +382,10 @@ static void dma_ctrl_orrc_cfg(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 	}
 }
 
-/* AVM hdma.c:577-617 verbatim. */
+/*
+ * AVM hdma.c:577-617, minus the descriptor-fetch-on-demand block, which
+ * AVM 08.25 moved to the tail of dma_ctrl_init (see there).
+ */
 static int dma_ctrl_cfg(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 {
 	int enable;
@@ -386,12 +396,6 @@ static int dma_ctrl_cfg(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 		enable = 0;
 	dma_ctrl_chan_flow_ctl_cfg(pctrl, enable);
 
-	if ((pctrl->flags & DMA_FTOD))
-		enable = 1;
-	else
-		enable = 0;
-	dma_ctrl_desc_fetch_on_demand_cfg(pctrl, enable);
-
 	if ((pctrl->flags & DMA_DESC_IN_SRAM))
 		enable = 1;
 	else
@@ -996,7 +1000,7 @@ static int dma_cfg_init(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 static int dma_ctrl_init(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 {
 	u32 i, j;
-	int ret;
+	int enable, ret;
 	struct dma_port *pport = NULL;
 	struct dmax_chan *pch = NULL;
 
@@ -1053,6 +1057,20 @@ static int dma_ctrl_init(struct dma_ctrl *pctrl)
 			}
 		}
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Descriptor fetch-on-demand goes on LAST, after every port and
+	 * channel is configured — AVM 08.25 hdma.c:3026-3030. It used to sit
+	 * in dma_ctrl_cfg, i.e. before the loops, which leaves the controller
+	 * free to fetch descriptors out of channels that have not been
+	 * programmed yet.
+	 */
+	if ((pctrl->flags & DMA_FTOD))
+		enable = 1;
+	else
+		enable = 0;
+	dma_ctrl_desc_fetch_on_demand_cfg(pctrl, enable);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
